1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
They are some of the most diabolical devices ever created.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Thousands of years old, they terrify and maim,

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
haunting their victims for a lifetime.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Used by governments and private citizens alike.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, Torture Devices on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,720
For at least 3,000 years, emperors and generals,

7
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:47,240
dictators and police, criminals, clerics,

8
00:00:47,241 --> 00:00:51,540
and even medical doctors have used a vast array of torture devices.

9
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:58,380
These insidious instruments included the mundane and the exotic.

10
00:00:58,381 --> 00:01:05,220
Everything from fists and feet to elaborate machines that literally slow-cooked their victims alive.

11
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:10,480
Wooden crosses were favored by the ancient Romans.

12
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:17,420
Various spiked metal encasements, stretching machines, and sexual devices by the medieval Europeans.

13
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,820
Fists, beatings, and electricity by 21st century torturers.

14
00:01:21,821 --> 00:01:32,700
When it comes to a definition of torture and torture devices, there is little disagreement

15
00:01:32,701 --> 00:01:33,700
among experts.

16
00:01:33,701 --> 00:01:42,000
Under the UN Convention Against Torture, which was passed by the General Assembly in 1984,

17
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:51,520
torture is defined as cruel, inhuman or degrading activities against an individual for the purpose

18
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:56,700
of extracting information or punishment or revenge or intimidation.

19
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:05,160
A torture instrument is essentially any instrument that's used to inflict forcible pain on a

20
00:02:05,161 --> 00:02:05,861
human being.

21
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,880
What kind of person tortures another human being?

22
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:15,200
And who among us would be inspired to create an instrument of torture and then actually

23
00:02:15,201 --> 00:02:15,901
use it?

24
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,540
People often imagine that the torture is some sort of psychopath.

25
00:02:20,541 --> 00:02:31,317
But in fact, torturers can be highly educated people who come out of universities, who have studied music and art and philosophy, and they can also be brutal people

26
00:02:31,318 --> 00:02:36,180
who come out of the prisons and are used by the state to act as torturers.

27
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:45,420
Over the past decade, the United Nations has openly denounced the use of any torture for any reason by any nation state.

28
00:02:45,421 --> 00:02:52,040
But what about cases like the capture of the al-Qaeda military commander, Khalid Sheikh Mohammed,

29
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:58,820
a man who was one of bin Laden's closest associates and al-Qaeda's number one strategist?

30
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:06,420
Would torture be justified in an attempt to extract vital information from them about future acts of terrorism?

31
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:14,980
Or to gain the names and locations of other terrorists in his vast network?

32
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:28,880
For anyone that thinks that torture is actually an effective device to get quality information,

33
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:36,060
that's certainly never been proven by any experience in humankind for centuries.

34
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:41,380
Torture gets people to say anything to make the pain go away.

35
00:03:41,381 --> 00:03:47,760
Experts tell us that the same is true when it comes to extracting confessions to a crime.

36
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:53,000
Confessions extracted under torture are notoriously bad.

37
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:58,800
I can get anyone to confess to anything.

38
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:04,840
If you give me enough time, I bet you I can get you to confess to anything I want you to confess to.

39
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:08,120
It's just a matter of time because people have a breaking point.

40
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:14,800
In fact, in early 2003, in the state of Illinois,

41
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:18,720
Governor George Ryan commuted the sentences of prison inmates

42
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:21,660
whose convictions were largely based on confessions

43
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:23,680
extracted as a result of torture.

44
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,140
Torture that included electronic shocking devices.

45
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:35,860
These electronic immobilization devices, or EIDs,

46
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,460
were originally developed in the 1970s.

47
00:04:38,461 --> 00:04:45,700
They were designed to be used as non-lethal restraint devices for police forces and prison guards who were dealing with dangerous criminals.

48
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:48,660
They are still lawful for this purpose.

49
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:58,979
But over the last 30 years, they have risen to prominence as illegal torture devices for a number of governments, military organizations, and police agencies who are

50
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,320
trying to hide their torture activities.

51
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:09,700
What a lot of the electric shack technology does now is it doesn't leave permanent marks.

52
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:18,140
And so it enables torturers to engage in or to inflict the same kind of intense pain

53
00:05:18,141 --> 00:05:23,340
to accomplish their objectives so they can deny that they engaged in torture.

54
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:29,500
Among the convenient electronic tools designed for one purpose

55
00:05:29,501 --> 00:05:31,740
but turned into a device for mistreating humans

56
00:05:31,741 --> 00:05:33,160
was the cattle prod,

57
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:36,120
which first appeared in the stockyards of Buenos Aires

58
00:05:36,121 --> 00:05:37,840
in the early 1930s.

59
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:44,560
It was nothing more than a billy club device

60
00:05:44,561 --> 00:05:46,960
that was initially used, quite literally,

61
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:49,760
to prod cattle to get them to move along.

62
00:05:49,761 --> 00:05:57,040
It was modified then into a device to prod people,

63
00:05:57,041 --> 00:06:01,980
a riot control device during the riots of the 1960s

64
00:06:01,981 --> 00:06:08,820
where you saw it used against people who were marching for their rights,

65
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:15,040
used by police forces in the South against poor black folks

66
00:06:15,041 --> 00:06:18,160
that were marching to be American citizens.

67
00:06:18,161 --> 00:06:27,420
A modern cattle prod delivers a mild shock at the point of physical contact

68
00:06:27,421 --> 00:06:31,500
by means of a low-voltage electric current sent across two electrodes.

69
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:37,060
Experts say the small amount of electricity does not harm cattle,

70
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:41,420
but it does deliver a reasonably painful shock to the smaller human body.

71
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:45,420
Stun guns are another story.

72
00:06:45,421 --> 00:06:59,421
A stun gun is a small, very concealable, handheld device that enables an individual who is in close physical proximity to another to deliver a debilitating charge of

73
00:07:01,759 --> 00:07:03,740
electricity to them.

74
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:11,760
You actually have to physically make contact with the individual and this device to generally incapacitate them.

75
00:07:11,761 --> 00:07:18,720
About the size of a typical flashlight, stun guns have a fairly simple design.

76
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:22,960
They generate a high-voltage, low-amperage electrical charge.

77
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:30,520
The power source is ordinary 9-volt batteries that supply electricity to a circuit that includes multiple transformers.

78
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:37,820
These components boost the voltage in the circuit anywhere from 80,000 volts to a high of 720,000 volts,

79
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:41,360
enough electricity to trigger 1,000 garage door openers.

80
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:55,760
Stun belts are basically stun guns that are attached to prisoners by means of a heavy-duty, one-size-fits-all belt.

81
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:08,400
There is a remote control unit that activates this device from as far a distance as 200 to 300 feet.

82
00:08:08,401 --> 00:08:15,580
When it's engaged, a stun belt will administer a high-voltage charge to the inmate's kidneys.

83
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:20,640
An instantaneous shock spreads throughout the entire body,

84
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:23,560
and the prisoner becomes paralyzed for several minutes.

85
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:30,240
The immediate effects of electric torture can include rupturing of blood vessels

86
00:08:30,241 --> 00:08:34,960
and tearing and rupturing of muscles and ligaments off the bone.

87
00:08:34,961 --> 00:08:40,080
The long-term effects really depend on the system of the body that's affected.

88
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:46,020
It's the kind of pain that causes people to urinate or defecate without control,

89
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:48,260
to lose control of their muscles.

90
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:55,300
It's the kind of pain that causes people to have ongoing mental health problems.

91
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:03,900
In some countries, these more sophisticated electronic immobilization devices are difficult to obtain.

92
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:09,120
As a result, torturers will use a cheap makeshift version,

93
00:09:09,121 --> 00:09:13,320
a couple of high-voltage batteries connected to a few cables or wires.

94
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:19,900
One of the ways that torture has often been perpetrated,

95
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:22,600
particularly against political dissidents,

96
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:28,980
has been the use of electricity where electrical wires have been attached to genitals

97
00:09:28,981 --> 00:09:33,100
or fingers or toes or a person's head,

98
00:09:33,101 --> 00:09:37,940
using a generator or a battery or any sort of electrical device.

99
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:42,600
Often what happens is that a person is splashed with water beforehand

100
00:09:42,601 --> 00:09:47,220
so that the electrical current will run easier into the body.

101
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:54,360
The use of these devices to terrorize, to punish,

102
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:58,740
to extract information and confessions leaves no visible trace.

103
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:02,900
But the torture instruments of antiquity were not so deceptive.

104
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:12,420
The Immigration and Naturalization Service estimates that not only do 400,000 torture

105
00:10:12,421 --> 00:10:19,360
victims live in the United States, but between 800 to 1,000 of their torturers do as well.

106
00:10:19,361 --> 00:10:24,900
Torture devices will return on Modern Marvels.

107
00:10:24,901 --> 00:10:34,040
In the 21st century, governments claim that the practice of torture is despicable and horrifying.

108
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:38,900
But for at least three millennia, torture was legal.

109
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:45,640
It was a commonly used method of punishment, not only for criminals, but also for innocent slaves and foreigners.

110
00:10:45,641 --> 00:10:50,780
over 3,000 years ago

111
00:10:50,781 --> 00:10:52,660
the Chinese book of wisdom

112
00:10:52,661 --> 00:10:53,800
known as the I Ching

113
00:10:53,801 --> 00:10:56,500
spoke of a punishment called the Shi Ho

114
00:10:56,501 --> 00:10:57,340
in this way

115
00:10:57,341 --> 00:11:00,480
Shin Ho, biting through

116
00:11:00,481 --> 00:11:02,940
his neck is locked in the wooden Kong

117
00:11:02,941 --> 00:11:04,560
his ears are gone

118
00:11:04,561 --> 00:11:05,900
great misfortune

119
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:10,840
this punishment required a device called the Kong

120
00:11:10,841 --> 00:11:13,880
it was used for minor criminal offenses

121
00:11:13,881 --> 00:11:15,340
well into the 20th century

122
00:11:15,341 --> 00:11:22,340
The Kong was a very large, heavy, thick wooden board with a hole cut in the middle the size of the victim's neck.

123
00:11:22,341 --> 00:11:28,340
This bulky device was worn for as long as 20 or 30 days.

124
00:11:28,341 --> 00:11:31,340
It was never allowed off the victim's neck.

125
00:11:31,341 --> 00:11:36,340
Because of the board's size, it was impossible for the victim to feed himself,

126
00:11:36,341 --> 00:11:40,340
making him dependent upon the kindness of others for food and drink.

127
00:11:40,341 --> 00:11:43,340
If he had no friends, he could die from starvation.

128
00:11:45,340 --> 00:11:55,240
In 1300 BC, in the Mediterranean world, the Egyptian pharaoh Ramses II is said to have

129
00:11:55,241 --> 00:12:00,340
tortured Hittite prisoners by flogging them with whips made of ox leather, weighted with

130
00:12:00,341 --> 00:12:05,180
bone splitters or metal hooks and spikes.

131
00:12:05,181 --> 00:12:10,020
His purpose was to extract information about their battle plan against Egypt and the location

132
00:12:10,021 --> 00:12:15,660
of their forces.

133
00:12:15,661 --> 00:12:20,800
The ancient Assyrians also practiced horrifying brutality, not just in warfare, but against

134
00:12:20,801 --> 00:12:23,960
their own dissident citizens.

135
00:12:23,961 --> 00:12:29,040
We have, for example, an ancient story of a village that tried to revolt against the

136
00:12:29,041 --> 00:12:30,820
Assyrian Empire.

137
00:12:30,821 --> 00:12:35,720
And the Assyrians rounded up the leaders of the revolt, flayed them alive, spread their

138
00:12:35,721 --> 00:12:39,800
skins on the city walls as a warning.

139
00:12:39,801 --> 00:12:43,560
We know this story from Assyrian sources.

140
00:12:43,561 --> 00:12:46,800
In other words, they wanted the story to be known.

141
00:12:46,801 --> 00:12:52,320
They wanted people to hear the horrific results of any who would oppose them.

142
00:12:52,321 --> 00:12:55,940
That is torture and terrorism.

143
00:12:55,941 --> 00:13:02,160
Nearly a thousand years later, it was the Greeks who brought their own brand of deadly

144
00:13:02,161 --> 00:13:08,160
ingenuity to the creation of one particular torture device, the brazen bull.

145
00:13:08,161 --> 00:13:14,240
This was a large copper bronze statue of a bull that was hollow.

146
00:13:14,980 --> 00:13:17,100
And according to the legend, according to the story,

147
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:22,240
you would place someone inside this device and then heat it.

148
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:27,100
The heat was created by fires that were lit underneath the hollow bronze figure,

149
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:29,840
making the brazen bull function like an oven.

150
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:33,580
The torture victim would slowly roast,

151
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:37,360
as well as suffocate from the loss of oxygen caused by the tremendous heat.

152
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:42,260
supposedly the groans of the person suffering inside

153
00:13:42,261 --> 00:13:45,720
would sound like the sound of a bull, the sound of a cow.

154
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:47,440
It's a horrific thought.

155
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:52,240
A few centuries later,

156
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,760
the Romans became known for using torture with a vengeance

157
00:13:55,761 --> 00:13:57,460
throughout their far-flung empire.

158
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:01,840
One of their most used torture devices was the flogging whip,

159
00:14:02,540 --> 00:14:04,260
also known as the flagellum.

160
00:14:04,261 --> 00:14:11,260
Its thongs were made from tanned ox leather, the ends of which were weighted with lead balls.

161
00:14:11,261 --> 00:14:18,260
As the torturer whipped his prisoner, these lead weights cut deeply and painfully into his victim's flesh.

162
00:14:18,261 --> 00:14:24,260
It's the lacerations themselves that probably hurt the most in whipping and caning.

163
00:14:24,261 --> 00:14:31,260
It's the tearing of the flesh against the bone below that hurt so much.

164
00:14:31,261 --> 00:14:38,140
It was during the reign of Tiberius that the most famous torture victim of all time, Jesus

165
00:14:38,141 --> 00:14:40,100
of Nazareth, was crucified.

166
00:14:40,101 --> 00:14:45,760
Dr. David C. Crucifixion as a form of public execution predates the Romans.

167
00:14:45,761 --> 00:14:53,600
We know at least as early as the Persian Empire some kind of public display of death that

168
00:14:53,601 --> 00:14:55,740
involves hanging up victims.

169
00:14:55,741 --> 00:14:59,880
The idea seems rather straightforward.

170
00:14:59,881 --> 00:15:07,380
a person's death as publicly horrific as possible.

171
00:15:07,381 --> 00:15:12,720
The purpose of this was to send a powerful message to would-be dissidents and troublemakers.

172
00:15:12,721 --> 00:15:17,360
Do not defy the state's authority.

173
00:15:17,361 --> 00:15:22,740
Hundreds of years before Jesus' torture and execution, victims of crucifixion were tied,

174
00:15:22,741 --> 00:15:28,000
not nailed, to a single wooden stake that was planted firmly in the ground.

175
00:15:28,001 --> 00:15:30,400
They were left to die from thirst or from starvation.

176
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:35,040
And there was always the possibility they would be attacked by hungry wild animals,

177
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:36,700
searching for an easy meal.

178
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:44,000
In approximately 70 B.C., the Romans introduced the four-pointed cross

179
00:15:44,001 --> 00:15:46,300
that would later become the symbol of Christianity.

180
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:49,300
But long before it was used to crucify Jesus,

181
00:15:49,940 --> 00:15:52,580
the Roman army made extensive use of their new cross

182
00:15:52,581 --> 00:15:57,800
when they crushed the slave rebellion led by the gladiator Spartacus in 71 B.C.

183
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,880
6,000 of his followers were crucified.

184
00:16:02,420 --> 00:16:05,460
The Roman commander had their bodies hung from beams

185
00:16:05,461 --> 00:16:08,160
that lined the road from Capua, where the revolt began,

186
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:12,460
to Rome, a distance of 270 miles.

187
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:16,140
Spartacus himself was killed in the battle.

188
00:16:17,460 --> 00:16:20,860
When we think about who it was that Rome executed

189
00:16:20,861 --> 00:16:25,000
in these horrific public forum, like the crucifixion,

190
00:16:25,620 --> 00:16:26,700
it's clear to us.

191
00:16:26,701 --> 00:16:33,720
the Romans executed publicly anyone who was seen to oppose Roman power.

192
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:38,700
Jesus of Nazareth was considered just such a threat.

193
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:41,520
He was arrested during Passover,

194
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:44,820
quickly tried and then convicted in the local Roman court.

195
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:49,060
It was then that he was subjected to a series of tortures.

196
00:16:50,060 --> 00:16:53,140
There's little reason to doubt the New Testament picture

197
00:16:53,141 --> 00:16:58,840
that Jesus began suffering long before the actual crucifixion,

198
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:01,540
that Jesus was whipped, that Jesus was beaten,

199
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:08,900
that he was already cut and bleeding from crowns of thorns or from the whipping

200
00:17:08,901 --> 00:17:14,380
and was made exhausted to carry the device of his own death.

201
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:19,920
The flogging whip used on Jesus was the lead-weighted leather flagellum,

202
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:22,980
the crown of thorns that was placed on his head.

203
00:17:23,140 --> 00:17:27,060
may have been constructed from the branches of a plant with long curved thorns,

204
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:31,780
called the Zisyphus spinocristi, that grows in the Jordan Valley.

205
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:36,760
His final torture took place on the wooden cross.

206
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:41,960
Crucifixion was designed to make death extended.

207
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:48,200
That is, it was to inflict the maximum amount of pain and suffering,

208
00:17:48,201 --> 00:17:50,360
and to make it last.

209
00:17:51,180 --> 00:17:54,900
Once at Golgotha, the location chosen for his crucifixion,

210
00:17:55,620 --> 00:17:58,380
iron spikes were driven through Jesus' wrists and feet.

211
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:03,960
There was nothing in place to support his shoulders, neck, head, or the bulk of his body.

212
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:08,360
As a result of this positioning, the uppermost portion of his trunk

213
00:18:08,361 --> 00:18:12,720
eventually would collapse over his diaphragm, making normal breathing impossible.

214
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:18,200
The best guess seems to be that people die from asphyxiation,

215
00:18:18,201 --> 00:18:23,860
unable to exhale because of the paralysis of the pectoral and intercostal muscles.

216
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:31,700
So their lungs become filled with carbon dioxide and unable to take in oxygen,

217
00:18:31,701 --> 00:18:34,760
and eventually they suffocate.

218
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:40,900
The Romans continued crucifying and torturing Christians for the next 300 years.

219
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:48,020
Then, ironically, the Roman Empire under Constantine embraced Christianity as its own state religion.

220
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:57,620
But as the years passed, Christianity itself adopted the use of torture, sending the imaginations

221
00:18:57,621 --> 00:19:04,880
of inventors of torture devices to new levels of darkness.

222
00:19:04,881 --> 00:19:08,900
The term torture was first used in 16th century France.

223
00:19:08,901 --> 00:19:14,100
It comes from the Latin word tortus, which means twisted.

224
00:19:14,101 --> 00:19:16,540
Torture devices will return on Modern Marvels.

225
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:24,900
The creation of tribunals to suppress opposing viewpoints has a long tradition.

226
00:19:26,740 --> 00:19:32,540
Clerics, kings, and even United States senators have employed state-sponsored courts or inquisitions

227
00:19:32,541 --> 00:19:35,260
to root out the heretics and non-believers.

228
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:42,940
In the 15th century, it was the Spanish royals, Isabella and Ferdinand,

229
00:19:42,941 --> 00:19:47,640
who gave what became known as the Spanish Inquisition its place in history.

230
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:51,860
has a harsh and deadly exercise of combined church and state power,

231
00:19:52,600 --> 00:19:55,820
dedicated to torturing those citizens who it named as heretics.

232
00:19:57,420 --> 00:20:02,920
The state, or the secular, has always been involved in torture to some capacity.

233
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:07,100
When we think about the Inquisition, we often associate it with the church.

234
00:20:07,101 --> 00:20:12,640
But the Inquisition was really the church and state working hand in hand,

235
00:20:12,820 --> 00:20:16,760
often for the purposes of the state more than the church.

236
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:26,700
Under the guise of establishing unity in her homeland, Queen Isabella asked for and received

237
00:20:26,701 --> 00:20:33,400
the Roman Catholic Pope's permission to purify her subjects.

238
00:20:33,401 --> 00:20:38,200
Spanish citizens of every rank and class had three choices.

239
00:20:38,201 --> 00:20:44,280
To accept Roman Catholicism as their religion, to leave Spain, or to risk torture at the

240
00:20:44,281 --> 00:20:45,440
hands of the Inquisition.

241
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:52,920
It only took two adult men to identify somebody as a heretic.

242
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:55,120
At that point, they could be tried.

243
00:20:55,800 --> 00:20:59,720
But before a trial could begin, a confession from the accused was required.

244
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:02,780
That's where torture became necessary.

245
00:21:03,780 --> 00:21:06,680
A typical torture session during the time of the Inquisition

246
00:21:06,681 --> 00:21:10,820
would often involve a number of different tools that would be present.

247
00:21:10,821 --> 00:21:14,200
And there might be a number of other people present participating.

248
00:21:14,201 --> 00:21:23,240
And it's very also important to note that there would be other victims who were both in the process of being tortured or yet to be tortured.

249
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:31,420
The face of torture during the Inquisition was the Iron Maiden, manufactured in Germany.

250
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:36,680
It was a man-sized container fitted on the inside with sharp metal spikes,

251
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:42,800
a torture device that was well-known and deeply feared not only in Spain but in all of Europe.

252
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:51,580
There was a great deal of craftsmanship, ingenuity, and knowledge of the human body to produce and design the Iron Maiden.

253
00:21:51,900 --> 00:22:00,420
As you open up, you'll notice that these spikes inside are placed in very strategic points intended to miss the vital organs.

254
00:22:00,860 --> 00:22:08,320
This was done to ensure the victim would remain alive as long as possible, all the while experiencing tremendous pain and suffering.

255
00:22:08,321 --> 00:22:13,820
While the Iron Maiden may have been the face of the Inquisition,

256
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:17,840
it was generally thought that the rack was its workhorse.

257
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:21,780
The rack is one of the most common forms of torture that we know

258
00:22:21,781 --> 00:22:28,480
in terms that you can use many different methods in your process, in your torture creation.

259
00:22:28,880 --> 00:22:32,420
The first element is the crank at the end.

260
00:22:32,421 --> 00:22:35,160
Now here is where you would tie the person's feet.

261
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:37,720
You would slowly add more tension.

262
00:22:37,721 --> 00:22:40,920
to the point of elongating the body.

263
00:22:41,460 --> 00:22:43,740
They would be rolled across these spikes

264
00:22:43,741 --> 00:22:46,680
that would be much sharper, of course, at the time.

265
00:22:47,300 --> 00:22:51,160
And then at this end, the hands would be locked down

266
00:22:51,161 --> 00:22:53,840
and really leaving the victim exposed,

267
00:22:54,460 --> 00:22:56,000
and often, of course, naked.

268
00:22:56,580 --> 00:22:59,320
And at that point, any number of things can be done.

269
00:23:01,640 --> 00:23:03,260
If the rack were to continue,

270
00:23:03,261 --> 00:23:09,580
there would be potentially ripping of tendons and ligaments and nerve damage

271
00:23:09,581 --> 00:23:13,740
that might be chronic and go on forever.

272
00:23:14,380 --> 00:23:20,220
People might feel the inability to use their hands or their lower extremities.

273
00:23:20,340 --> 00:23:24,460
They might feel numbness, constant pain in their fingertips or toes.

274
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:29,740
Ultimately, the rack was designed to slowly tear the victim's limbs out of their joints.

275
00:23:29,741 --> 00:23:33,600
the legs out of the hip joints, the arms out of the shoulder joints.

276
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:38,480
The design of torture instruments changed as technology became more advanced.

277
00:23:39,160 --> 00:23:44,360
During the 1800s, a chair, completely forged of hardened metal, made its appearance.

278
00:23:44,980 --> 00:23:48,880
Simply called the interrogation chair, it soon became common throughout Europe.

279
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:52,780
The primary function would be to sit you down.

280
00:23:52,880 --> 00:23:56,260
You're sitting on these spikes, which are obviously not comfortable.

281
00:23:57,260 --> 00:23:59,600
This bar would lock down your chest.

282
00:24:00,240 --> 00:24:02,400
Your wrists would be held down as well.

283
00:24:02,860 --> 00:24:04,840
And then your legs would be clamped.

284
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:11,460
Once sitting in the chair, chances are it's not going to be too long before you begin to talk.

285
00:24:11,461 --> 00:24:16,240
I would guess that certain people would begin to talk before they sat in the chair.

286
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:18,840
I mean, it's very ominous. You wouldn't want to be here.

287
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:23,800
The second major torture mechanism and design of the chair, actually, is that it can be heated.

288
00:24:23,801 --> 00:24:28,240
All the metal spikes that you see conduct heat.

289
00:24:28,660 --> 00:24:33,460
Fire would be placed underneath the chair, bringing this intense, hot feeling.

290
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:38,960
Thumbscrews and finger screws were another highly engineered torture device.

291
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:43,740
Machine-tooled to mechanical precision, they worked like a pair of nutcrackers.

292
00:24:44,200 --> 00:24:50,220
The way it would be used would be to place the fingers or knuckles inside the two bars,

293
00:24:50,221 --> 00:24:57,300
slowly increasing pressure until the proper amount of pain was necessary

294
00:24:57,301 --> 00:25:00,460
to get the information that you might want from the victim.

295
00:25:00,461 --> 00:25:03,240
The thumb is the most important finger you have.

296
00:25:03,660 --> 00:25:09,280
So to actually crush the thumb makes you almost useless as a manual laborer.

297
00:25:09,460 --> 00:25:11,840
So it's got tremendous value in that way.

298
00:25:12,140 --> 00:25:15,360
It's also a very sensitive part of your body.

299
00:25:15,361 --> 00:25:24,140
The nerves are highly dense in the fingers, so that crushing nerves in the fingers causes significant pain.

300
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:30,180
A few torture devices seem to go beyond ordinary tools of pain and suffering.

301
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:34,580
Perhaps the most dreadful instrument of such debauchery was the pair.

302
00:25:35,400 --> 00:25:40,040
What's amazing about this piece in particular is the amount of work that went into it.

303
00:25:40,900 --> 00:25:41,960
It's made of bronze.

304
00:25:42,120 --> 00:25:42,820
It's cast.

305
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:45,660
And it took a bit of ingenuity and technology,

306
00:25:45,820 --> 00:25:46,940
especially for the time period.

307
00:25:47,660 --> 00:25:50,640
Like many torture instruments, they're multifunctional.

308
00:25:51,700 --> 00:25:55,160
In an oral capacity, it would be placed into the mouth

309
00:25:55,161 --> 00:26:01,420
and slowly opened for those who had uttered heretical comments.

310
00:26:02,180 --> 00:26:06,660
Now, for those who had committed homosexuality,

311
00:26:06,661 --> 00:26:08,800
it would be placed in the rectum.

312
00:26:08,801 --> 00:26:11,520
and again slowly opened, increasing the pain.

313
00:26:12,180 --> 00:26:15,380
In the case of women, it'd be placed in the vagina,

314
00:26:15,860 --> 00:26:17,660
and again slowly opened,

315
00:26:18,600 --> 00:26:23,520
and to the point where these points would puncture the uterus.

316
00:26:26,740 --> 00:26:28,920
Across the Atlantic, in the New World,

317
00:26:29,680 --> 00:26:32,720
Native American nations had mastered the use of torture devices

318
00:26:32,721 --> 00:26:35,820
that were far less elaborate than the Iron Maiden or even the RAC.

319
00:26:35,821 --> 00:26:41,900
But the basic reasons for torturing prisoners remained the same, to punish and to terrorize.

320
00:26:42,820 --> 00:26:49,900
In the East Coast, we do find lots of evidence of using shells to flay people alive, usually while they're tied to a stake.

321
00:26:50,340 --> 00:26:56,040
And oftentimes that was followed by burning or boiling the stripped flesh in front of the victim.

322
00:26:57,060 --> 00:27:01,460
Among the Algonquin, it was the men who would capture the victims and tie them up.

323
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:05,140
But it was often left to the women to perform the actual torture.

324
00:27:05,820 --> 00:27:11,600
Some of the burning was akin to what was happening in Europe

325
00:27:11,601 --> 00:27:13,080
in terms of burning at the stake.

326
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:18,000
Having a low heat fire slowly smolder under the victim's feet.

327
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:21,820
Other times the torturers would insert pieces of wood

328
00:27:21,821 --> 00:27:24,280
into various parts of the body and set them on fire.

329
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:27,760
There's accounts of people placing them under the fingernails,

330
00:27:27,900 --> 00:27:31,040
in the mouth, in the ears, in the nose,

331
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:34,500
anywhere we could have a non-fatal burn.

332
00:27:34,501 --> 00:27:37,120
so to prolong the agony of the victim.

333
00:27:37,880 --> 00:27:39,780
Very often, the length of the torture itself

334
00:27:39,781 --> 00:27:41,460
was determined by the victim's behavior.

335
00:27:42,080 --> 00:27:44,520
There was honor for those who stoically accepted the pain.

336
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:49,160
It's the people who cried out, who expressed some sort of fear.

337
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:51,700
They could expect a much longer torture session

338
00:27:51,701 --> 00:27:53,400
before ultimately being killed,

339
00:27:53,800 --> 00:27:56,220
but usually death was the ultimate result.

340
00:27:57,980 --> 00:28:02,100
By the mid-19th century, in Europe, Great Britain, and the United States,

341
00:28:02,101 --> 00:28:05,540
there was a groundswell of popular sentiment against torture.

342
00:28:07,320 --> 00:28:10,860
In 1864, the Geneva Convention was drafted.

343
00:28:11,920 --> 00:28:16,180
It was an international agreement that, among other things, prohibited the use of torture.

344
00:28:18,280 --> 00:28:23,940
But rather than bringing the practice to an end, it would slowly drive it underground.

345
00:28:23,941 --> 00:28:32,720
The Inquisition, along with its use of torture devices, was not officially abolished by the

346
00:28:32,721 --> 00:28:39,360
Vatican until 1834. Torture devices will return on Modern Marvels.

347
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:51,420
By World War II, the practice of torture had been illegal for almost 80 years.

348
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:57,560
The creators of torture devices should have been out of business.

349
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:01,560
Instead, they turned to everyday appliances,

350
00:29:02,320 --> 00:29:04,820
items that could be found in the household or on the job.

351
00:29:05,320 --> 00:29:08,460
The amazing thing is the common devices,

352
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:11,400
the things that you use in your everyday life,

353
00:29:11,540 --> 00:29:16,160
things that you find at home that become suddenly violent,

354
00:29:17,060 --> 00:29:19,000
sick devices of torture.

355
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:23,820
A fork can be used to hold somebody's finger in place,

356
00:29:23,940 --> 00:29:28,020
to hold somebody's pinky in place, to stab somebody.

357
00:29:28,680 --> 00:29:31,520
The same with the, you know, the pliers.

358
00:29:31,980 --> 00:29:36,200
I mean, again, they're so simple, and yet they can create such agony.

359
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:41,480
All of these things were used as torture devices by the Nazis, by the Gestapo.

360
00:29:42,760 --> 00:29:45,820
A famous French-born British spy named Odette

361
00:29:45,821 --> 00:29:49,040
was arrested by the Gestapo in Marseille in 1943.

362
00:29:49,041 --> 00:29:54,840
When she refused to answer her interrogator's questions about the French underground and

363
00:29:54,841 --> 00:30:00,140
the British-supported spy network operating in Vichy, France, Odette's torture began.

364
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:07,180
They took her to an interrogation room, and they used a red-hot poker, and they touched

365
00:30:07,181 --> 00:30:14,220
it to her third or fourth lumbar on her spine and just held it there, burning her skin for

366
00:30:14,221 --> 00:30:21,800
up to minutes and they pulled her fingernails out very slowly, fingers and toenails, one

367
00:30:21,801 --> 00:30:26,020
at a time, with pliers.

368
00:30:26,021 --> 00:30:31,880
In spite of the wretched pain and heavy discharge of blood, Odette refused to give up her comrades,

369
00:30:31,881 --> 00:30:36,220
who she knew faced certain death.

370
00:30:36,221 --> 00:30:40,800
Even though she was herself sentenced to death, Odette somehow turned up in a concentration

371
00:30:40,801 --> 00:30:47,400
camp for women and survived the war.

372
00:30:47,401 --> 00:30:51,460
Most Gestapo torture victims knew they were being tortured because they had verbal secrets

373
00:30:51,461 --> 00:30:54,660
their torturers wanted.

374
00:30:54,661 --> 00:30:58,760
But a small group of victims at Auschwitz concentration camp had no idea that they were

375
00:30:58,761 --> 00:31:05,400
being tortured because they had medical secrets locked away in their bodies.

376
00:31:05,401 --> 00:31:09,400
the notorious Dr. Josef Mengele tortured his victims in the name of science.

377
00:31:10,860 --> 00:31:14,720
The devices he employed were the common tools of a physician,

378
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:17,700
scalpels, forceps, and surgical scissors.

379
00:31:18,820 --> 00:31:25,920
Really, he's remembered for his grotesque and vicious torture on the inmates,

380
00:31:26,540 --> 00:31:30,200
particularly twins, identical as opposed to fraternal.

381
00:31:30,201 --> 00:31:33,760
He would then perform various experiments.

382
00:31:33,761 --> 00:31:42,100
Some involved injecting different chemicals into the body of one twin and not the other and see how they react.

383
00:31:43,020 --> 00:31:48,560
He also wanted to take tissue samples from the major organs.

384
00:31:49,360 --> 00:31:53,280
And this was often done, you know, in the most brutal way.

385
00:31:55,880 --> 00:32:02,660
One of Mengele's most horrifying tortures was placing some twins into a vat of near-boiling water for a period of time

386
00:32:02,661 --> 00:32:05,000
and then strapping them onto a table

387
00:32:05,001 --> 00:32:07,280
so that the softened tissue samples

388
00:32:07,281 --> 00:32:09,020
could be more easily sliced away

389
00:32:09,021 --> 00:32:11,280
without the benefit of anesthesia.

390
00:32:12,080 --> 00:32:15,900
There were 125 sets of twins in Auschwitz in 1944.

391
00:32:16,900 --> 00:32:20,000
By the end of the year, only a dozen or so survived.

392
00:32:20,740 --> 00:32:22,720
This is how monstrous a man,

393
00:32:23,280 --> 00:32:26,700
the experiments and all of these things that he performed,

394
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:28,780
this is his legacy, really.

395
00:32:28,781 --> 00:32:30,520
And of course, he's never caught.

396
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:32,040
I mean, he got away.

397
00:32:32,660 --> 00:32:37,020
Mengele vanished in 1945 when Allied forces liberated the camps.

398
00:32:37,760 --> 00:32:41,460
Despite international efforts to track him down, he was never apprehended.

399
00:32:42,420 --> 00:32:49,060
A 68-year-old man who died in Brazil in 1979 was later identified through DNA as Mengele.

400
00:32:55,420 --> 00:33:00,660
In the Pacific during World War II, wherever the Imperial Japanese forces were victorious,

401
00:33:00,661 --> 00:33:03,660
the people of that nation were often tortured.

402
00:33:03,661 --> 00:33:08,660
It made no difference if the victims were civilians or military POWs.

403
00:33:08,661 --> 00:33:15,660
The most common instruments of punishment were the canes and whips they used to flog their prisoners.

404
00:33:15,661 --> 00:33:24,660
Some POWs were hung upside down by their feet, while urine nor iodine was poured directly into their nostrils or down their throats.

405
00:33:24,661 --> 00:33:26,203
their throats.

406
00:33:26,204 --> 00:33:35,020
In January 1942, when the Japanese invaded the American-held Philippine Islands,

407
00:33:35,760 --> 00:33:39,693
American civilian expatriates were arrested and imprisoned.

408
00:33:39,694 --> 00:33:41,760
The first civilian the Japanese

409
00:33:41,761 --> 00:33:47,040
took into custody was a middle-aged, stubborn Kentuckian who had been slightly crippled from

410
00:33:47,041 --> 00:33:47,741
polio.

411
00:33:47,742 --> 00:33:53,140
His name was Roy Bennett, a respected journalist who had refused to stop criticizing

412
00:33:53,141 --> 00:33:55,500
the Japanese government's violent empire building.

413
00:33:57,880 --> 00:34:00,300
His wife and two daughters were also arrested

414
00:34:00,301 --> 00:34:03,660
and placed in the Santo Tomas internment camp in Manila.

415
00:34:05,440 --> 00:34:09,220
He had been the editor of the Manila Daily Bulletin,

416
00:34:09,221 --> 00:34:13,040
which was the most influential English-language newspaper

417
00:34:13,041 --> 00:34:14,140
in the Far East.

418
00:34:14,940 --> 00:34:18,240
And he, as an influential person,

419
00:34:18,420 --> 00:34:20,680
saying negative things against the Japanese,

420
00:34:20,681 --> 00:34:25,140
And he was very harsh, very loud, prior to their coming in.

421
00:34:26,020 --> 00:34:32,100
Bennett was incarcerated in Fort Santiago, an ancient dreary prison reserved for civilian political prisoners.

422
00:34:32,380 --> 00:34:39,000
It had been a fortress built by the Spanish when they were in charge of the Philippines, when they owned the Philippine Islands.

423
00:34:39,580 --> 00:34:45,880
And it was exactly what you would think of as a Spanish dungeon and fortress.

424
00:34:45,881 --> 00:34:50,800
Bennett's Japanese captors wanted to use him as a propaganda tool

425
00:34:50,801 --> 00:34:54,040
to torture him into admitting he had been gravely misguided

426
00:34:54,041 --> 00:34:56,180
when he spoke out against the Japanese government

427
00:34:56,181 --> 00:35:01,560
One of the tortures that was used on many of the political prisoners

428
00:35:01,561 --> 00:35:06,720
was inserting a bamboo sliver deep under the fingernail

429
00:35:06,721 --> 00:35:08,060
and then lighting it

430
00:35:08,061 --> 00:35:11,540
but probably the most common physical torture

431
00:35:11,541 --> 00:35:14,240
was simply beating them

432
00:35:14,241 --> 00:35:18,800
The beatings were administered by just about anything at hand, including their hands.

433
00:35:19,620 --> 00:35:25,940
They would use clubs, bamboo canes, the flat side of a bayonet,

434
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:32,500
just anything that they could use to inflict great pain.

435
00:35:33,500 --> 00:35:37,660
As Bennett and other civilian, political, and military prisoners suffered,

436
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:42,000
the Japanese were also torturing Filipinos who remained loyal to the Americans.

437
00:35:42,001 --> 00:35:48,080
It was not uncommon for a Japanese soldier to take a baby from its mother's arms,

438
00:35:48,620 --> 00:35:53,360
from its Filipino mother's arms, throw it in the air and catch it on the bayonet.

439
00:35:54,320 --> 00:35:59,700
It was not uncommon for the soldiers to rape the women,

440
00:36:00,740 --> 00:36:06,060
to execute or torture a Filipino man in front of his family,

441
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:10,880
or to torture the Filipino wife and children in front of the man.

442
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:19,240
On February 3, 1945, Roy Bennett and his family were among the prisoners liberated by the American forces.

443
00:36:21,620 --> 00:36:24,340
Two weeks later, they returned to the United States.

444
00:36:25,220 --> 00:36:30,140
The torture really never ends. It is always with the person who's been tortured.

445
00:36:31,340 --> 00:36:33,138
They relive it all the time.

446
00:36:33,139 --> 00:36:37,120
They have to decide why it is that they lived and others died.

447
00:36:37,121 --> 00:36:45,800
the screams are as real to them 20 years after the fact as they were when it was happening to them.

448
00:36:47,260 --> 00:36:53,840
According to his family, Roy Bennett carried the psychological scars of his torture until his death in 1967.

449
00:36:57,000 --> 00:37:02,140
35 years later, and 3,000 miles southwest by way of the South China Sea,

450
00:37:02,141 --> 00:37:04,720
a gruesome discovery was made.

451
00:37:04,721 --> 00:37:12,000
It was in Cambodia in 1978 that the invading Vietnamese army found Pol Pot's secret prison.

452
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:14,720
He called it S-21.

453
00:37:15,900 --> 00:37:21,620
It was used as a secure location to torture Cambodian prisoners he believed were counter-revolutionaries.

454
00:37:22,020 --> 00:37:26,420
This was a secret place because the whole business of what the government was doing

455
00:37:26,421 --> 00:37:29,620
was kept a secret for most of the people in the country most of the time,

456
00:37:29,621 --> 00:37:32,980
particularly as if it involved torturing and killing people

457
00:37:32,981 --> 00:37:35,360
who were accused of being enemies of the state.

458
00:37:36,200 --> 00:37:38,880
A prison archive was meticulously maintained.

459
00:37:39,860 --> 00:37:43,640
Within its pages are entries that reveal appalling acts of torture

460
00:37:43,641 --> 00:37:46,080
carried out against its inmate population.

461
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:53,020
What you have here is an interrogator's appendix to a confession.

462
00:37:53,200 --> 00:37:54,800
In other words, it's a rare kind of document.

463
00:37:54,801 --> 00:37:57,580
You get very few of these documents where an interrogator tells you

464
00:37:57,581 --> 00:38:00,180
what he did in sequence.

465
00:38:01,580 --> 00:38:04,800
On the morning of the 18th of August, 1977,

466
00:38:05,740 --> 00:38:07,300
I decided, this is the interrogator speaking,

467
00:38:07,400 --> 00:38:08,540
I decided to employ torture.

468
00:38:09,240 --> 00:38:11,820
I watched his morale fall while I administered torture,

469
00:38:11,920 --> 00:38:12,760
but he had no reaction.

470
00:38:13,300 --> 00:38:16,020
In the afternoon of the 21st, another day later,

471
00:38:16,280 --> 00:38:18,760
I pressured him again using the electric cord.

472
00:38:20,160 --> 00:38:23,080
He was given two or three spoonfuls of s*** to eat.

473
00:38:23,700 --> 00:38:24,760
That would be human excrement.

474
00:38:24,761 --> 00:38:27,340
And after that, he was able to answer questions.

475
00:38:27,580 --> 00:38:31,580
That night I beat him again with the electric cord.

476
00:38:31,581 --> 00:38:34,580
It's an incredible quotation as you see.

477
00:38:34,581 --> 00:38:38,580
Besides being forced to eat feces,

478
00:38:38,581 --> 00:38:41,580
prisoners also were forced to drink urine,

479
00:38:41,581 --> 00:38:44,580
were subjected to electric shocks

480
00:38:44,581 --> 00:38:47,580
and suffocation with a plastic bag.

481
00:38:47,581 --> 00:38:53,580
One of the many clients that I have worked with from Cambodia,

482
00:38:53,581 --> 00:38:54,357
Cambodia.

483
00:38:54,358 --> 00:39:01,700
He had a plastic bag put over his head during the course of an interrogation session.

484
00:39:02,260 --> 00:39:05,320
It was put over his head. They would ask him a question. He would not answer.

485
00:39:05,900 --> 00:39:09,050
They would put it over his head, and he would nearly suffocate.

486
00:39:09,051 --> 00:39:10,600
They would take it off briefly,

487
00:39:11,100 --> 00:39:13,500
interrogate him some more, put the bag over his head.

488
00:39:13,501 --> 00:39:20,340
The consequence of being in a bag is asphyxiation.

489
00:39:21,640 --> 00:39:25,040
The person who's asphyxiated loses consciousness.

490
00:39:25,640 --> 00:39:28,980
They may lose consciousness and come back too, and the torture continues.

491
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:34,720
There may be a cycle of asphyxiation, losing consciousness, and coming back for more torture.

492
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:38,800
They also have the possibility of being asphyxiated so badly that they die.

493
00:39:38,801 --> 00:39:44,680
The long-term consequences of asphyxiation would most likely include brain damage.

494
00:39:46,620 --> 00:39:52,080
In 1984, four years after the discovery of the horror of Pol Pot's secret prison,

495
00:39:52,980 --> 00:39:56,880
the United Nations General Assembly passed the UN Convention Against Torture.

496
00:39:57,900 --> 00:40:02,100
Even so, today the worldwide use of torture and torture devices

497
00:40:02,101 --> 00:40:05,180
continues to spin dangerously out of control.

498
00:40:08,800 --> 00:40:13,060
Nearly 15,000 Cambodians were incarcerated in Pol Pot's S21 prison.

499
00:40:13,980 --> 00:40:16,160
Only six survived the torture.

500
00:40:17,780 --> 00:40:20,060
Torture devices will return on Modern Marvels.

501
00:40:23,160 --> 00:40:28,600
Experts tell us that torture and the use of torture devices is currently a worldwide epidemic.

502
00:40:30,180 --> 00:40:37,160
According to Amnesty International, 75% of the world's governments have employed torture since the beginning of the new millennium.

503
00:40:37,161 --> 00:40:43,160
Electronic shocking devices, beatings, and psychological abuse top the list.

504
00:40:43,161 --> 00:40:48,160
And there is an ever-increasing array of ordinary objects that are not technology-driven,

505
00:40:48,161 --> 00:40:53,160
but that are used as instruments of torture simply because they are available.

506
00:40:53,161 --> 00:40:57,160
Among the most disturbing may be sexual torture.

507
00:40:57,161 --> 00:41:04,160
Vaginal penetration, sodomization, men and women are subjected to rape as a form of torture.

508
00:41:04,161 --> 00:41:09,160
A famous example of that is the Coke bottle.

509
00:41:09,161 --> 00:41:14,160
Coca-Cola bottles were used in Latin America to be inserted in victims' anuses.

510
00:41:14,161 --> 00:41:14,861
Why?

511
00:41:14,862 --> 00:41:19,160
Because when the person was let go from the prison, the Coca-Cola bottle is ubiquitous.

512
00:41:19,161 --> 00:41:24,160
They could not go anywhere without seeing it and therefore being reminded of their torture.

513
00:41:24,161 --> 00:41:31,160
The most accessible contemporary torture devices are fists, feet, whips, and canes.

514
00:41:31,161 --> 00:41:31,861
canes.

515
00:41:31,862 --> 00:41:36,280
It may be for this reason that beatings are among the most common form of torture in

516
00:41:36,281 --> 00:41:41,740
the world today, especially where there is religious persecution, in countries like Egypt,

517
00:41:42,060 --> 00:41:44,265
Pakistan, the Sudan, and China.

518
00:41:44,266 --> 00:41:48,960
One famous form of torture that occurred for a long time and still

519
00:41:48,961 --> 00:41:54,780
occurs throughout the world is falanga, which is the beating on the soles of the feet with a blunt

520
00:41:54,781 --> 00:41:56,163
object like a cane.

521
00:41:56,164 --> 00:42:01,260
What happens short term is tremendous pain and bleeding in the bottoms

522
00:42:01,261 --> 00:42:02,105
of the feet.

523
00:42:02,106 --> 00:42:07,740
But after that heals, for months and even years afterwards, the torture survivor

524
00:42:07,741 --> 00:42:12,100
has pain just walking on their feet.

525
00:42:12,101 --> 00:42:15,479
Beatings are sometimes accompanied with degrading acts of obscenity.

526
00:42:15,480 --> 00:42:16,820
A somewhat typical incident

527
00:42:16,821 --> 00:42:20,180
involved three Ethiopian women.

528
00:42:20,181 --> 00:42:23,800
Most of all, they were stripped of their clothing.

529
00:42:23,801 --> 00:42:28,560
They were hung upside down by a pole, just an ordinary pole.

530
00:42:28,561 --> 00:42:31,900
And then they were whipped with cords.

531
00:42:31,901 --> 00:42:35,920
When they screamed, they would have socks put in their mouths.

532
00:42:35,921 --> 00:42:39,840
Sometimes they would be bloody socks, sometimes vomit-filled socks.

533
00:42:39,841 --> 00:42:44,120
And this is a very typical form of torture.

534
00:42:44,121 --> 00:42:52,200
In my experience, there are a wide range of implements and other objects that have been

535
00:42:52,201 --> 00:42:54,400
used to torture individuals.

536
00:42:54,401 --> 00:43:01,940
To name a few, iron bars, sometimes that are heated up and applied to the body, cigarette

537
00:43:01,941 --> 00:43:07,360
butts, batons are quite common, rifle butts.

538
00:43:07,361 --> 00:43:12,200
I've had clients who have been, their bodies have been pressed against hot burning radiators.

539
00:43:14,200 --> 00:43:19,860
Often the key to the successful use of a torture device is the torturer's skill in keeping his victim alive.

540
00:43:21,900 --> 00:43:25,840
Persons who have been tortured, certainly those who are being tortured for political purposes,

541
00:43:26,200 --> 00:43:32,200
are sent out into the community as symbols of what will happen to others if they continue to be in opposition.

542
00:43:32,800 --> 00:43:34,140
You need to be alive for that.

543
00:43:34,141 --> 00:43:37,960
and you need for the community to see you as a broken person.

544
00:43:38,800 --> 00:43:42,220
The use of torture devices remains a shadowy business.

545
00:43:43,440 --> 00:43:46,540
While civilization's enemies may be arrested and tortured,

546
00:43:47,500 --> 00:43:50,260
legions of innocents are left to suffer the same fate,

547
00:43:50,900 --> 00:43:52,440
sometimes for years on end.

548
00:43:53,220 --> 00:43:55,500
With the threat of violence on the increase,

549
00:43:56,280 --> 00:43:59,780
it is likely the use of torture devices will increase as well.

550
00:44:00,940 --> 00:44:03,660
Ultimately, what people can really do to help

551
00:44:03,661 --> 00:44:06,980
resolve the issue of torture in our world

552
00:44:06,981 --> 00:44:10,720
is to gain a new understanding of it,

553
00:44:11,240 --> 00:44:13,100
to accept that it exists,

554
00:44:13,540 --> 00:44:14,840
and to become educated.

555
00:44:15,940 --> 00:44:17,080
Because it's real.

556
00:44:17,540 --> 00:44:19,680
And the more educated we are about it,

557
00:44:19,820 --> 00:44:23,960
the more likely we are to do what we can to resolve it.

558
00:44:24,320 --> 00:44:26,000
This is a dark side of our humanity

559
00:44:26,001 --> 00:44:28,020
that we need to deal with and better understand.

